  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "lesson-01",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. Start with native McStas syntax\n",
    "\n",
    "A McStas instrument is a text file named `something.instr`. McStas translates it to C, compiles that C, and runs the resulting ray-tracing program. The major blocks are:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Syntax | Purpose |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| `DEFINE INSTRUMENT` | Instrument name and input parameters, with types and defaults |\n",
    "| `DECLARE %{ ... %}` | C variables shared by the instrument |\n",
    "| `INITIALIZE %{ ... %}` | Calculations and checks performed once before tracing |\n",
    "| `TRACE` | Ordered components encountered by neutron rays |\n",
    "| `COMPONENT name = Type(...)` | One named instance of a library component |\n",
    "| `AT (...) RELATIVE name` | Position in another component's local coordinate frame |\n",
    "| `ROTATED (...) RELATIVE name` | Orientation relative to a component frame; angles are degrees |\n",
    "| `EXTEND %{ ... %}` | Custom C actions at a trace point, such as absorbing an unwanted ray |\n",
    "| `FINALLY %{ ... %}` | Optional reporting or cleanup at the end |\n",
    "| `END` | End of instrument definition |\n",
    "\n",
    "The contents of `%{ ... %}` are C, so statements there need semicolons. McStas geometry uses metres; wavelength is in angstroms, time in seconds, and frequency in hertz. C `sin()` and `cos()` use radians. Convert degrees using `DEG2RAD`. The name after `COMPONENT` is the instance name: later `RELATIVE` statements refer to that name, not the component type."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "77c8efcd",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
