  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "lesson-04",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. The major instrument components\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Source:** samples the initial position, direction, wavelength, emission time, and statistical weight. Here we use `Source_pulsed` with a moderator pulse model.\n",
    "- **Guide:** transports neutrons by reflection from coated surfaces. A plain `Guide` is useful for simple stages; `Elliptic_guide_gravity` describes the shaped FANTASTIC guide.\n",
    "- **Disk chopper:** a rotating aperture. The PSC shapes the time pulse; the BC selects a wavelength/time band and suppresses frame overlap.\n",
    "- **Arm:** a coordinate frame only. It helps place an exit plane, sample, analyser, or detector in a readable hierarchy.\n",
    "- **Sample:** scatters, changes neutron energy, and changes ray weight according to a sample model. A ray that was not scattered can be absorbed with an `EXTEND` rule.\n",
    "- **Curved monochromator:** an analyser crystal that reflects a selected final wavelength by Bragg scattering. Its mosaic and curvature control angular acceptance and focus.\n",
    "- **Slit and monitor:** a `Slit` clips rays outside an aperture; monitors record wavelength, time, position, or other ray properties. `restore_neutron=1` lets a diagnostic monitor record a ray and continue tracing it.\n",
    "\n",
    "In the native file, the trace order and `AT`/`ROTATED` statements define the geometry. McStasScript writes this same instrument structure from Python, which is convenient for parameter sweeps and repeated channels.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Monitors: how to inspect the instrument while building it\n",
    "\n",
    "A monitor observes selected neutron properties at a component location. It normally records a histogram and, with `restore_neutron=1`, lets the neutron continue so you can compare several positions in one run.\n",
    "\n",
    "- `L_monitor` bins wavelength. Use it to check the source band and confirm whether a chopper or filter changes the transmitted wavelength range.\n",
    "- `TOF_monitor` bins arrival time. Put one before and after a chopper to check its timing window.\n",
    "- `PSD_monitor` bins horizontal and vertical position. Use it to inspect the beam footprint at a guide exit or sample plane.\n",
    "- `Monitor_nD` is the flexible monitor used for the spectrometer tubes. Its `options` string selects the geometry and axes to bin. Here the tube configuration uses a cylindrical tube axis, two vertical bins, and 1000 time bins. Each tube writes a separate ASCII `.dat` file.\n",
    "\n",
    "For a first debug run, use monitors at boundaries between subsystems: after the source, after a guide or chopper, at the sample, and in the detector tubes."
   ]
  },
