    "## 6. Build 3: complete the FANTASTIC primary spectrometer\n",
    "\n",
    "The final production beamline is a separate instrument from the PSC/BC timing exercise. It has **no choppers**: set the source to \\(\\lambda_{min}=2\\) Å and \\(\\lambda_{max}=4\\) Å. The source is 0.5 m before a 35 m elliptic gravity guide; the guide exit is 1.5 m before the sample. Therefore\n",
    "\\[\n",
    "L_i=d_{source,guide}+L_{guide}+d_{guide,sample}=0.5+35+1.5=37\\;\\mathrm{m}.\n",
    "\\]\n",
    "Use the project focal dimensions: `linx=0.611423`, `liny=1.04627`, `loutx=1.68246`, and `louty=1.94531` m. The guide mid-section is 0.12 m × 0.12 m, with `dimensionsAt=\"mid\"`, elliptic option, gravity enabled, coating `m=4`, and reflectivity `R0=0.99`. Add an `Arm` at the guide exit and another at the sample plane. Verify the beam with monitors before adding secondary channels.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why the production beamline is a fresh instrument\n",
    "\n",
    "The first two builds isolate chopper timing. The production instrument instead uses a pulsed source whose wavelength limits already select the desired 2–4 Å band, followed by one uninterrupted guide. Start a new `McStas_instr` so no teaching PSC/BC components or their monitor groups remain in the production model.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Verify the primary geometry before adding a sample\n",
    "\n",
    "With the source at (z=0), the guide entrance is at (z=0.5) m and its exit is at (z=35.5) m. The sample arm is 1.5 m farther downstream, at (z=37.0) m. A wavelength monitor at the guide exit checks the transported band; a PSD monitor there checks the beam position and size."
   ]
