    "## 7. Build 4: create one analyser wedge (one channel)\n",
    "\n",
    "An analyser channel is the unit repeated around the spectrometer. It contains a position/orientation arm, a curved monochromator, a fixed detector arm, a collimating slit, and two detector tubes. Build one channel and check that it reflects toward its detector before generating 29 more.\n",
    "\n",
    "For first-order Bragg reflection,\n",
    "\\[\n",
    "\\lambda_f=2d\\sin A_5,\\qquad A_5=\\sin^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\lambda_f}{2d}\\right).\n",
    "\\]\n",
    "The reflected ray deflects by \\(2A_5\\). For an analyser centre at horizontal channel angle \\(\\alpha\\) and radius \\(d_{sa}\\),\n",
    "\\[\n",
    "x=d_{sa}\\sin\\alpha,\\qquad z=d_{sa}\\cos\\alpha.\n",
    "\\]\n",
    "The arm rotation sets the analyser scattering plane; the additional monochromator rotation `-A5` sets the crystal Bragg orientation. Focus radii for the FANTASTIC convention are \\(R_H=-d_{sa}\\) and \\(R_V=-2d_{sa}\\sin A_5\\).\n",
    "\n",
    "### What one wedge teaches\n",
    "\n",
    "A channel is a small secondary spectrometer in its own local coordinate frame. The analyser arm puts the crystal at its sample-to-analyser position. The monochromator then selects the outgoing wavelength by Bragg reflection. A detector arm and plane define the fixed FANTASTIC detector geometry; a slit limits accepted rays; two adjacent `Monitor_nD` components represent the two tubes in the channel. Build these one at a time and inspect them before making a loop.\n",
    "\n",
    "The reference monochromator uses `order=1`, which selects its first Bragg reflection; `order=0` allows all orders, including higher-order reflection of shorter wavelengths such as $\\lambda_f/2$. A practical instrument may also use a beryllium filter in the secondary path to suppress short-wavelength contamination. This teaching geometry leaves that filter out as an optional component for students to add and test."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "lesson-23",
   "metadata": {},
