    "### Add the sample and focus it on one analyser face\n",
    "\n",
    "The one-channel reference uses a rectangular sample focus that covers the projected analyser face. For analyser width $w_a$, height $h_a$, Bragg angle $A_5$, and sample distance $d_{sa}$, use\n",
    "\\[\n",
    "s_f=\\frac{d_{sa}}{d_{sa}-\\tfrac12 w_a\\cos A_5},\\qquad\n",
    "w_{f,x}=h_a s_f,\\qquad w_{f,y}=w_a\\sin A_5\\,s_f.\n",
    "\\]\n",
    "The target point must be transformed from the instrument frame into the rotated sample's local frame. Record incident energy before scattering; for positive tunnelling loss, reject the gain branch and double the retained weights. In the full-bank instrument, replace this face focus with an angular focus aimed at the arc midpoint.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "The sample focus is a separate step from analyser placement. First define the sample geometry and rotation parameters. Then aim the sample's scattering focus at the analyser face, taking the inverse sample rotation when a target is specified in the sample's local coordinates. Finally add the scattering component and its post-scattering rule. Keep the sample in the instrument even when switching between sample models; replace only the component and its model-specific parameters.\n",
    "\n",
    "As an optional model exercise, replace `Tunneling_sample` with `Phonon_simple`. Use the lead preset from the reference instrument: radius 10 mm, height 10 mm, `sigma_abs=0.171` barn/atom, `sigma_inc=0.003` barn/atom, \\(a=4.9396\\) Å, \\(b=9.405\\) fm, \\(M=207.2\\) u, \\(c=12.90095\\) meV Å, `DW=1`, and \\(T=300\\) K. Aim its angular focus at the analyser-arc midpoint with a 120° horizontal and 4° vertical opening, and use an absolute sample rotation. `Phonon_simple` is a one-branch teaching model, not a quantitative lead-dispersion calculation."
   ]
