    "## 9. Test before running for long statistics\n",
    "\n",
    "Run a small neutron count first. Inspect the generated `.instr`, then check the source, guide-exit monitor, sample scattering, analyser placement, fixed detector direction, slit, and tube time range. Use `mcdisplay instrument.instr` for the standard geometry viewer or `mcdisplay-webgl instrument.instr` for an interactive 3D view in your web browser. Increase the neutron count only after the small run puts rays where expected.\n",
    "\n",
    "For ASCII output, use McCode text format, for example:\n",
    "\n",
    "```bash\n",
    "mcrun -n 100000 --format=McCode full_instrument.instr\n",
    "mcdisplay full_instrument.instr\n",
    "mcdisplay-webgl -n 100 full_instrument.instr\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "The WebGL command starts a local viewer and normally opens it in your default browser. Add the instrument's `name=value` parameters to either display command when you are not using defaults. A small `-n` is enough to inspect geometry and keeps the 3D view responsive.\n",
    "\n",
    "Keep each simulation in a separate run directory. If the executable is being recompiled, wait for compilation to finish before launching another run; otherwise a concurrent run can encounter “Text file busy”. A binary compiled for a different processor can also fail with “CPU ISA level is lower than required”; recompile on the target machine with compatible compiler settings.\n",
    "\n",
    "### A3 scan: reference workflow (do not run during the school)\n",
    "\n",
    "For a crystal scan, A3 is the sample rotation about the vertical (y)-axis, so the scanned parameter is `sample_rotation_y`; `sample_rotation_x` and `sample_rotation_z` stay at zero for this mounting. In McStas, `-N 100` requests 100 scan points and `-n` sets the ray count at every point. The command below records how the supplied data were produced, but **do not launch this full scan during the school**: simulating and reducing 100 angles with 60 detector files per angle is too computationally expensive.\n",
    "\n",
    "For reference, a production command would have the form:\n",
    "\n",
    "```bash\n",
    "mcrun -n 1000000 -N 100 --format=McCode --dir=A3_scan \\\n",
    "  FullStudent.instr sample_rotation_y=0,360 sample_rotation_x=0 sample_rotation_z=0\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "McStas writes one numbered run directory per scan point, and the saved `sample_rotation_y` value records its A3 angle. During class, limit yourself to one or two low-statistics angles if you want to check the scan syntax. For analysis, open `reduction_crystal/tubes_data_reduction_crystal.ipynb` and use the already processed `crystal1.parquet`, `crystal2.parquet`, and `crystal3.parquet` files. Install its third-party dependencies in the same environment that launches Jupyter:\n",
    "\n",
